  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Known as one of the Provence Landing sites (night of 14 to 15 August 1944, Commandos d'Afrique), Canadel beach is today a peaceful sandy beach, split into two zones by a lifeguard post. On the right, a stele commemorates the landing. The tourist office praises its fine sand continuing into clear water, with posidonia meadows and renowned seabeds. The beach is supervised in high season and open all year, with free access; it also has private beaches with catering, including the restaurant L'Ancre d'Or and the Tropicana Club cited by the tourist office.</p>
      <p>It is reached on foot or by bike along the Voie Verte, going down via avenue de la Méditerranée, avenue du Docteur Ratynski or avenue de France, or by car via a car park signposted on the D559, near the chapel, opposite the necropolis and the road to the Canadel pass. A bus stop is less than 500 m from the beach.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>From 1 June to 30 September, dogs on a leash are allowed on all the town's beaches between 7pm and 8am only; outside those hours, they are not allowed. This is what the town hall (news item of 23 June 2026 and the summer 2026 "Carnets du Rayol-Canadel-Pramousquier" bulletin) and the Gulf of Saint-Tropez tourist office state; the latter's beach listing, updated in 2026, says dogs are not admitted from 8am to 7pm during the summer period. Owners must pick up waste; identified guide and assistance dogs kept on a leash remain welcome. For the rest of the year (1 October to 31 May), neither the town nor the tourist office publishes a precise rule: ask the town hall before coming.</p>
      <p>As for facilities, the town mentions public showers and toilets on the Rayol and Canadel beaches, as well as a pontoon and an ecological mooring area for boats. Bathing water quality results are posted on each beach and at the town hall reception. Since a leash is required and the permitted slots are early morning and evening, bring fresh water and a shady spot for your dog, as the sand heats up quickly in summer.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the town has removed the bins from the beaches ("no bins directly on the beaches", summer 2026 bulletin) — bring bags and leave with your dog's waste.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The nearby Domaine du Rayol botanical garden, however, does not admit pets, even on a leash or in a backpack (Domaine's rules) — worth knowing if you plan to stop there on the way. <a href="/en/fiche-plage-pramousquier-rayol-canadel">Pramousquier beach</a> is 2 km away along the Voie Verte, the converted former railway line linking the commune's three hamlets (Le Rayol, Le Canadel and Pramousquier); the town describes it as a safe, flat route for walking or cycling.</p>
